! AD by the OTHER route: SIC storing below 19-bit address 16.0.
!
! WHY A SECOND PROGRAM AND NOT MORE LINES IN THE FIRST.  AD has two
! triggers in the model, and op_ad_indicators.strap only reaches one of
! them (a floating-point operand address beyond the installed core).
! The other is here.  It needs its own program because AD IS SET-ONLY:
! nothing in the model clears it, and BRANCH ON BIT cannot either --
! Reference Manual p.41 lets BB "test and change the contents of
! positions 20-63 of the indicator register", and AD is bit 16, inside
! the read-only 0-19 region.  So a second AD case appended to the first
! program would find AD already on and its on-check would prove
! nothing.  Starting from a fresh program is what makes the check
! discriminating -- the same reason the off-checks come first there.
!
! THE RULE, Reference Manual p.42, quoted in StretchExecute's SIC arm:
! "The instruction counter may be stored in any available location with
! 19-bit address 16.0 or above" -- below that, AD is set and NOTHING IS
! STORED.  Both halves are checked here.
!
! THE ONE-BIT TRICK.  $FT is word 14, the Factor register: below 16.0,
! freely readable and writable, and with no side effects of its own.
! Set its bit 0 to one, then aim SIC at it.  SIC stores the instruction
! counter into bits 0-18 of the target half-word, and the counter here
! is a small address whose top bit is 0, so a store that actually
! happened would CLEAR that bit.  One BZB then distinguishes "nothing
! was stored" from "something was" -- no compare, no second constant.
!
! WEAKER STANDARD, STATED: hand-written from the manual, not
! transcribed.  No deck block found so far aims a SIC below 16.0.
!
! BOTH HALVES DISCRIMINATE, measured separately, because one injection
! could not separate them: the obvious mutation (drop the ha < 32 test)
! both skips AD and performs the store, so it only proves whichever
! check comes first.
!
!   AD never set (if (ha < 32) -> if (False))    halts 002404.00, the
!                                                  BZB,$AD,$
!   AD set but the store happens anyway          halts 002405.00, the
!     (an extra writeWord inside the AD branch)    BZB,$FT,$
!
! AND ONE INJECTION THAT PASSED, which says something about the model
! rather than about this program.  Relaxing the READ-bail guard from
! "o.taken && ha >= 32" to "ha >= 0" -- so SIC below 16.0 goes and
! fetches its target word before discarding it -- changes nothing
! observable, because the AD branch downstream suppresses the store
! regardless.  That guard is an optimisation, not a correctness
! requirement: it avoids a pointless read of a special register.  Worth
! knowing before anyone "simplifies" it and expects a test to notice.

        SLC, (8)2400.0
START   BB, $AD, $                   ! hang up if AD on -- it must start off
        BB1, $FT, $+1.0              ! set Factor bit 0 to one
        BZB, $FT, $                  ! hang up if that did not take
        SIC, $FT                     ! store IC below 16.0 -> AD, no store
        B, ADSIC                     !   the branch SIC prefixes; taken
ADSIC   BZB, $AD, $                  ! hang up if AD off
        BZB, $FT, $                  ! hang up if the bit was cleared:
                                     !   nothing may have been stored
        B, PASS
PASS    BEW, $
        END, START
